<article class="env-lemma" id="00RY">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00RY">Lemma <span data-tag="00RY">10.131.15</span>.</a> Suppose $R \to S$ is of finite presentation. Then $\Omega _{S/R}$ is a finitely presented $S$-module. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Write $S = R[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]/(f_1, \ldots , f_ m)$. Write $I = (f_1, \ldots , f_ m)$. According to Lemma <a href="/tag/00RU" data-tag="00RU">10.131.9</a> there is an exact sequence of $S$-modules 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  I/I^2 \to \Omega _{R[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]/R} \otimes _{R[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]} S \to \Omega _{S/R} \to 0  \]
</div>
    <p>
       The result follows from the fact that $I/I^2$ is a finite $S$-module (generated by the images of the $f_ i$), and that the middle term is finite free by Lemma <a href="/tag/00RX" data-tag="00RX">10.131.14</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>